                         le.yang@canterbury.ac.nz                                    yl.xu@sjtu.edu.cn                               junsun@sjtu.edu.cn\n                                                        University of Canterbury                              Shanghai Jiaotong University                     Shanghai Jiaotong University\n                                                      Christchurch, New Zealand                                     Shanghai, China                                  Shanghai, China\n\n                                           Abstract                                                                                ACM Reference Format:\n                                           Point cloud compression (PCC) is critical for efficient storage and                     Xueqin Niu, Mufan Liu, Yifan Wang, Le Yang, Yiling Xu, and Jun Sun. 2026.\n                                                                                                                                   ResPCC: A Loss-Resilient Neural Point Cloud Codec over Lossy Networks.\n                                           transmission of 3D data. While recent learning-based PCC meth-\n                                                                                                                                   In Proceedings of the 34th ACM International Conference on Multimedia (MM\n                                           ods achieve good rate-distortion (R-D) performance, they generally\narXiv:2608.11845v1 [eess.IV] 12 Aug 2026\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                                                                   ’26), November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil. ACM, New York, NY, USA,\n                                           rely on ideal transmission conditions. In practice, packet loss is a                    18 pages. https://doi.org/10.1145/3767308.3835677\n                                           common issue and can severely distort latent features, causing co-\n                                           ordinate drift and geometric degradation. To address this challenge,\n                                           we present ResPCC, the first end-to-end neural point cloud codec\n                                           designed to offer intrinsic resilience against data loss. Our frame-\n                                           work is loss-rate-aware and adapts to diverse packet loss conditions.\n                                           At the encoder, we introduce a Condition-Adaptive Latent Modula-\n                                           tion (CALM) module to adjust latent feature distributions according\n                                           to the perceived loss rate, as well as a Spatial-Channel Interleaving                              GT               Ours (2.200 Bpp)      DPCC (2.293 Bpp)\n                                           (SCI) mechanism that transforms channel-wise data extinction into\n                                           spatially scattered element-wise missing patterns. At the decoder,\n                                           we develop a Mask-Aware Graph-based Latent Restoration (MGLR)\n                                           module, followed by a Dictionary-based Refinement (DBR) stage\n                                           to recover corrupted features and align them with canonical pri-\n                                           ors. Evaluations on ShapeNet and SemanticKITTI under 5% to 30%\n                                                                                                                                       GPCC (2.487 Bpp)     OctAttention (2.330 Bpp) PCGCv2 (2.275 Bpp)\n                                           packet loss rates show that ResPCC consistently delivers superior\n                                           stability and R-D performance over baselines. Our framework main-                       Figure 1: Impact of packet loss on the reconstruction quality\n                                           tains high reconstruction fidelity under lossy conditions, providing                    of existing codecs.\n                                           a reliable solution for 3D data transmission over practical networks.\n                                           Code is available at https://github.com/starrynight314/ResPCC.                          1    INTRODUCTION\n                                                                                                                                   Point clouds have become a fundamental representation for 3D\n                                           CCS Concepts                                                                            perception and communications [16, 40, 48]. However, their sub-\n                                           • Information systems → Multimedia streaming; • Networks                                stantial data volume often requires efficient compression before\n                                           → Error detection and error correction.                                                 storage and transmission. In practical network environments, the\n                                                                                                                                   resulting bitstreams are packetized and transmitted over lossy net-\n                                           Keywords                                                                                works, making them susceptible to packet loss. When packet loss\n                                                                                                                                   occurs, the received data may be incomplete, causing the recon-\n                                           Point cloud compression; Error concealment; Loss resilience\n                                                                                                                                   structed point cloud to exhibit geometric distortion and structural\n                                                                                                                                   degradation. Attaining both compression efficiency and robustness\n                                           ∗ These authors contributed equally to this work.                                       against packet loss is thus essential for reliable point cloud delivery.\n                                           † Corresponding author.\n                                                                                                                                      Over the years, point cloud compression (PCC) has been studied\n                                                                                                                                   under traditional and learning-based frameworks. Unlike images or\n                                                                                                                                   videos which have a fixed grid structure, point clouds are unordered\n                                                                                                                                   and irregular. As a result, PCC typically begins with organizing\n                                           This work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License.   the raw geometry into a more structured form. Traditional PCC\n                                           MM ’26, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil                                                          achieves this using handcrafted techniques, with MPEG G-PCC and\n                                           © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).\n                                           ACM ISBN 979-8-4007-2213-4/2026/11                                                      V-PCC [14, 34] as two representative standards based on hierarchi-\n                                           https://doi.org/10.1145/3767308.3835677                                                 cal partitions and projected patches. Learning-based PCC uses a\n\fMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil                                                                              Xueqin Niu et al.\n\n\nsimilar compression pipeline, but instead learns compact latent fea-            • Experiments show that a single ResPCC model generalizes\ntures from hierarchically downsampled point sets and reconstructs                 across diverse loss rates while maintaining strong rate-distortion\nthe geometry using a neural decoder [13, 21, 30, 37–39, 44].                      performance and competitive efficiency.\n    Most existing PCC methods are developed under the assumption\nthat the compressed bitstream is lossless during transmission. Their      2 RELATED WORKS\nperformance could degrade severely when packet loss occur. This           2.1 Point Cloud Compression\nis illustrated in Fig. 1. Traditional codecs, such as G-PCC, are highly\nsensitive to bitstream corruption. They may completely fail to de-        Traditional methods. Hierarchical tree structures are widely\ncode or exhibit the cliff effect [46], where even minor packet loss       used in traditional PCC. Early works employ KD-trees [10, 12]\nresults in missing regions in the reconstruction. For learning-based      and quadtrees [25], while octree-based methods [14, 28, 33, 34]\ncodecs, incomplete latent representations due to packet loss tend to      are particularly effective and forms the foundation of the MPEG\ndisrupt reconstruction more widely, resulting in sparsity artifacts       G-PCC standard [14, 34]. G-PCC achieves excellent performance\n(PCGCv2 [38]) or structural distortion (DPCC [18]). To mitigate           for sparse static point clouds. In contrast, V-PCC [14, 34] targets\nthese issues, existing solutions typically rely on either transmission    dense or dynamic sequences. Despite its efficiency, G-PCC is highly\nprotection or error concealment. Transmission protection schemes,         sensitive to packet loss due to its hierarchical dependencies and\nsuch as forward error correction (FEC) or automatic repeat request        state-dependent entropy coding.\n(ARQ), can protect the bitstream but introduce additional bandwidth          Learning-based methods. Deep learning has been widely ap-\noverhead or retransmission delay. Error concealment attempts to           plied in data compression [39, 45, 47] to learn compact represen-\ninfer missing content post-decoding, but lacks reliable priors for        tations. Learned PCC can be categorized into point-, voxel-, and\nfaithful restoration when packet loss causes global distortion [23].      octree-based methods [43]. Point-based methods operate on raw\nThese strategies implicitly treat robustness as an add-on feature         point coordinates, enabling continuous coordinate representation\nrather than an indispensable part of the codecs for point clouds,         and avoiding quantization artifacts. Existing methods typically\nleaving the packet-loss-aware design largely unexplored.                  adopt hierarchical autoencoder architectures [13, 21, 44] and often\n    This paper presents ResPCC, the first end-to-end neural point         employ patch-wise strategies [18, 22, 41, 47]. Our proposed ResPCC\ncloud codec with intrinsic resilience to packet loss for intra-frame      follows this point-based, patch-wise paradigm. Voxel-based methods\ngeometry compression. ResPCC accounts for packet loss directly            [15, 30, 39] map point clouds to volumetric grids, where sparse\nin the codec design across encoding, transmission, and decoding.          convolutions [8] and tensors [37, 38] mitigate computational costs.\nAt the encoder